      "title": "Disable named Service"
    },
    {
      "descriptions": [
        {
          "data": "If it is necessary for a secondary nameserver to receive zone data via zone transfer from the primary server, follow the instructions here. Use dnssec-keygen to create a symmetric key file in the current directory: This output is the name of a file containing the new key. Read the file to find the base64-encoded key string: Add the directives to on the primary server: Add the directives below to on the secondary nameserver:",
          "label": "default"
        },
        {
          "data": "The BIND transaction signature (TSIG) functionality allows primary and secondary nameservers to use a shared secret to verify authorization to perform zone transfers. This method is more secure than using IP-based limiting to restrict nameserver access, since IP addresses can be easily spoofed. However, if you cannot configure TSIG between your servers because, for instance, the secondary nameserver is not under your control and its administrators are unwilling to configure TSIG, you can configure an allow-transfer directive with numerical IP addresses or ACLs as a last resort.",
          "label": "rationale"
        },
        {
          "data": "The purpose of the dnssec-keygen command is to create the shared secret string base64-key-string. Once this secret has been obtained and inserted into named.conf on the primary and secondary servers, the key files Kdns.example.com .+NNN +MMMMM .key and Kdns.example.com .+NNN +MMMMM .private are no longer needed, and may safely be deleted.",
          "label": "warning"
        }
      ],
      "id": "xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_dns_server_authenticate_zone_transfers",
      "impact": 0.5,
